Interfaces actually have a runtime purpose: their declared methods serve as alternate names for public methods on their implementing classes. By convention, ASC will assign a special namespace for each interface, and any variable of that interface type will access traits using the interface namespace only.

For example...

```
interface ITest {
    function method();
}

class Test implements ITest {
    public function method() {
        trace("Called Test.method");
    }
}

var t = new Test();
var i : ITest = t;
```

In this example, ASC will synthesize a namespace string, and put all of `ITest`'s methods in that namespace. Calls to methods on `t` work normally, but calls to `i` are scoped to that namespace only. This is almost equivalent to this alternate example:

```
namespace ITest = "https://ruffle.rs/0db8/ITest";

class Test {
    public function method() {
        trace("Called Test.method");
    }

    ITest function method() {
        trace("Called Test.method");
    }
}

var t = new Test();
```

In this second example, `i.method()` becomes `t.ITest::method()`. However, note that we had to declare the method twice. With interfaces, the runtime is responsible for doing the aliasing, not the compiler. (This is also why you can't use namespaces in interfaces.)

This is literally the only function of interfaces that I could find, however. In fact, I'm pretty sure you can't even use interfaces to disambiguate between conflicting localnames like you can with, say, Rust traits.

